{"name":"Deep Amethyst","hex":"#5b3082","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"deep","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Extending, reaching or positioned far from a point of reference, especially downwards.\nExtending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively.","Extending far down from the top, or surface, to the bottom, literally or figuratively."]},{"part":"Adverb","definitions":["Far, especially far down through something or into something, physically or figuratively.","In a profound, not superficial, manner."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/deep"},{"term":"amethyst","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A transparent purple to violet variety of quartz with traces of manganese, used as a gemstone.","A purple colour."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a colour similar to that of the gemstone"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/amethyst"}],"entries":[{"title":"Amethyst","blocks":[{"text":"Amethyst is a violet variety of natural quartz. Ancient Greeks wore amethyst and carved drinking vessels from it in the belief that it would prevent intoxication. Amethyst, a semiprecious stone, is often used in jewelry. It occurs mostly in association with calcite, quartz, smoky quartz, hematite, pyrite, fluorite, goethite, agate, and chalcedony."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b5/Amatista_Laye_2.jpg/330px-Amatista_Laye_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amethyst","wikidata":"Q79058"},{"title":"Lavender (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Lavender is a light shade of purple or violet. It applies particularly to the color of the flower of the same name."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9b/Lavandula_fields.jpg/330px-Lavandula_fields.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedi